Restores the #620 defect: the stack guard becomes a direct call, so recursion
whose depth tracks the *spec's* structure can abort the process again.

This is the operator for the one failure mode the delivery layer cannot report.
An abort returns neither the JSON envelope nor an exit code, so it does not
produce a wrong verdict -- it produces no verdict at all, and every detector
that reads stdout or `$?` sees nothing to disagree with. The primary detector
therefore asserts on `Output::status.code()` being `Some`, not on a value.

The patch is one line at the single place the guard lives, which is exactly
why the guard was consolidated there: an operator that had to patch eight call
sites would be calibrating the patch rather than the defect.

--- a/rust/fsl-syntax/src/recursion.rs
+++ b/rust/fsl-syntax/src/recursion.rs
@@ -53,5 +53,6 @@
 /// directly, which is exactly today's behavior there. Browser-side depth stays
 /// bounded by the host, as before.
 pub fn guard<R>(body: impl FnOnce() -> R) -> R {
-    stacker::maybe_grow(RED_ZONE, NEW_STACK_SIZE, body)
+    let _ = (RED_ZONE, NEW_STACK_SIZE);
+    body()
 }
